00:06:18.658 --> 00:06:19.450
JEFF TURNHAM: Sure.

00:06:19.450 --> 00:06:22.780
So just as a reminder, so SoFy,
short for solution factory,

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.540
provides to our end users
and business partners

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:27.268
a catalog of our software.

00:06:27.268 --> 00:06:28.060
They can browse it.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:30.550
They can learn about
everything in the catalog.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.160
And then they can deploy it.

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:33.910
And they actually have
a couple of options

00:06:33.910 --> 00:06:35.110
when it comes to deployment.

00:06:35.110 --> 00:06:37.840
They can basically
take away a package

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.990
and go install it into their
own cloud native environment

00:06:40.990 --> 00:06:44.650
or what we find gets the
most excitement is, anything

00:06:44.650 --> 00:06:48.220
in our catalog, you can click
on and try out in a few minutes.

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.140
And the way that's
done is we actually

00:06:50.140 --> 00:06:53.970
do a real Kubernetes
deploy when the user says

00:06:53.970 --> 00:06:55.220
they want to deploy something.

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.950
So our architecture
works like this.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:07:00.130
When our users first
come to our website,

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:03.340
they are hitting a
GCP load balancer.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:06.490
That load balancer is
protected with Cloud Armor

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:10.660
so malicious traffic won't
even get to our application,

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:11.530
typically.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:14.440
So when traffic does
get to our application,

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:17.530
then it's hitting what we
call the SoFy cluster, which

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.180
is where our SoFy
application runs,

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.170
the application that provides
the catalog and all of the web

00:07:23.170 --> 00:07:25.510
experience that the users get.

00:07:25.510 --> 00:07:27.490
So as users browse
around the catalog,

00:07:27.490 --> 00:07:30.380
they're interacting with
our SoFy application.

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:32.620
And then when a user
wants to deploy something

00:07:32.620 --> 00:07:35.170
quickly and try it
out in a few minutes,

00:07:35.170 --> 00:07:37.330
they basically press
a deploy button.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:39.430
And that kicks off
under the covers

00:07:39.430 --> 00:07:42.280
a Helm install that
will go install

00:07:42.280 --> 00:07:44.950
what they've selected out
to what we call our sandbox

00:07:44.950 --> 00:07:45.930
cluster.

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:49.030
This cluster is a
very large cluster

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:52.427
that has all kinds of
various node pools in it.

00:07:52.427 --> 00:07:54.760
We have Windows node pools
because some of our workloads

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:55.570
are Windows.

00:07:55.570 --> 00:07:58.840
We have custom node pools for
some particular workloads that

00:07:58.840 --> 00:08:00.290
need to run in a certain way.

00:08:00.290 --> 00:08:03.730
And then we have the generic
Linux node pools and hundreds

00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:04.990
of nodes.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:08.830
And so when a user
installs, their application

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:10.300
gets installed in this cluster.

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:13.637
And they get back
network endpoints

00:08:13.637 --> 00:08:15.970
that they can connect to a
few minutes later, typically.

00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:18.980
So they'll be able to log
into our software, try it out,

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.710
and so on.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:25.060
And across all of this
is all of the images

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:26.350
that are getting pulled.

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:27.850
All the container
images that are

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.433
getting pulled into
these environments

00:08:29.433 --> 00:08:33.580
are getting pulled from Google's
container registry, GCR.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:38.260
And for when we want to deliver
software to our end users,

00:08:38.260 --> 00:08:41.020
we actually use another
image registry for that.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.800
We're using Harbor, very popular
open source image registry.

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:48.308
And we run that in yet
another GKE cluster.

00:08:48.308 --> 00:08:50.350
And then across all of
these environments-- and I

00:08:50.350 --> 00:08:53.852
should say this environment
pictured here is

00:08:53.852 --> 00:08:55.060
just one of our environments.

00:08:55.060 --> 00:08:56.450
It's our production environment.

00:08:56.450 --> 00:09:00.370
We obviously have internal
environments for our dev teams,

00:09:00.370 --> 00:09:03.470
for test teams, for performance,
for security, for staging.

00:09:03.470 --> 00:09:06.940
So we basically clones of
this throughout our work.

00:09:06.940 --> 00:09:09.370
All of these environments
are protected

00:09:09.370 --> 00:09:13.390
with various levels of security
but also observability,

00:09:13.390 --> 00:09:16.570
and monitoring, and alerting
for when things go wrong.

00:09:16.570 --> 00:09:19.810
And we also have
backup done with Valero

00:09:19.810 --> 00:09:22.940
that backs up to Google storage.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.270
And we can restore in
the case of emergency

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:26.810
very easily that way.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:27.310
So yeah.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:30.160
That's basically the
architecture in a nutshell.

00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.980
ROMIN IRANI: Thanks, Jeff,
for taking us step by step

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.330
through the architecture.

00:09:34.330 --> 00:09:37.960
And it's amazing to see that,
beneath all that complexity,

00:09:37.960 --> 00:09:40.180
the experience that
you've given to the users

00:09:40.180 --> 00:09:43.190
is a single click to
deploy the applications.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:45.640
What I'd like to do,
as you are talking

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:48.730
through the architecture,
is specifically,

00:09:48.730 --> 00:09:52.150
from a GKE perspective,
which forms really

00:09:52.150 --> 00:09:54.730
the core of your
platform, could you

00:09:54.730 --> 00:09:58.120
tell us specifically a
few things around GKE

00:09:58.120 --> 00:10:01.150
and what particularly was well
suited to your applications?

00:10:01.150 --> 00:10:04.660
Plus I'd like to touch a little
bit on the whole security

00:10:04.660 --> 00:10:07.650
aspect with Cloud Armor and
how that service has also

00:10:07.650 --> 00:10:08.658
helped you.

00:10:08.658 --> 00:10:09.450
JEFF TURNHAM: Sure.

00:10:09.450 --> 00:10:10.350
So yeah.

00:10:10.350 --> 00:10:13.740
When I think about GKE,
the term that comes to mind

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.420
is progressive disclosure.

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:19.020
And I mean that in the
nicest possible way.

00:10:19.020 --> 00:10:21.810
As a new user, when
you try out GKE,

00:10:21.810 --> 00:10:23.970
creating your first cluster
is just a few clicks.

00:10:23.970 --> 00:10:27.180
There's a whole bunch of
stuff beneath the surface

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:29.470
there for when you need it.

00:10:29.470 --> 00:10:32.820
And so for us in particular,
our operations team

00:10:32.820 --> 00:10:37.020
needs to understand at the most
detailed level possible what's

00:10:37.020 --> 00:10:39.240
going on in our
clusters, whether that's

00:10:39.240 --> 00:10:41.100
the SoFy cluster that
runs our application,

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.610
or the sandbox cluster that
runs all of the user deployed

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.310
applications, or whether that's
Harbor, our image registry.

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:51.000
And so what we really appreciate
is that observability,

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:54.695
and monitoring, and logging
is just built into everything.

00:10:54.695 --> 00:10:56.820
We didn't have to do anything
special to enable it.

00:10:56.820 --> 00:11:00.000
It's just there waiting for us
to run monitoring and logging

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:01.450
queries and get all that data.

00:11:01.450 --> 00:11:05.160
So we built up an entire suite
around those capabilities

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:07.230
where we have alerts,
which will kick off

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.660
when certain events
happen, things like a node

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:14.500
going bad or pods going
unhealthy, things like that.

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:17.230
So we have all kinds of
stuff built around that.

00:11:17.230 --> 00:11:19.140
And I'd say across--

00:11:19.140 --> 00:11:21.900
to your security comment,
I'd say across the board,

00:11:21.900 --> 00:11:26.310
our general experience is when
we need to use a GCP service,

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:27.550
it's quite straightforward.

00:11:27.550 --> 00:11:30.450
So taking the example
of Cloud Armor.

00:11:30.450 --> 00:11:34.050
I think it probably
took us an hour maybe

00:11:34.050 --> 00:11:37.680
to install Cloud Armor for
our initial prototypes.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:39.480
And most of that was
just reading the docs

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:40.890
to understand what it was doing.

00:11:40.890 --> 00:11:43.500
The actual enablement of
it with the load balancer

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:45.510
and the appropriate
back end and our cluster

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.935
was really, really
straightforward.

00:11:47.935 --> 00:11:49.560
And then we also had
to do some testing

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:53.010
to make sure malicious
payloads are getting

00:11:53.010 --> 00:11:54.520
blocked and things like that.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:55.020
But yeah.

00:11:55.020 --> 00:11:58.950
The documentation, the defaults,
they were all set and tuned

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:00.570
very well for our use case.

00:12:00.570 --> 00:12:02.460
And then when we do
need to make changes,

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:04.470
as we mentioned earlier,
it's all in the docs

00:12:04.470 --> 00:12:06.070
and very easy to understand.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.460
So I think that's
really why we like

00:12:08.460 --> 00:12:11.010
using GCP so much is the
services are there when we need

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.200
them and the
capability is there,

00:12:13.200 --> 00:12:18.690
even if it's behind a very
nice easy to use facade.

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:21.210
There's a whole bunch of
interesting stuff waiting

00:12:21.210 --> 00:12:22.063
to be discovered.

00:12:22.063 --> 00:12:23.730
ROMIN IRANI: The other
interesting thing

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:27.100
I'd like to ask is that,
as you built this platform,

00:12:27.100 --> 00:12:30.930
I'm sure there were certain
points in time where you had

00:12:30.930 --> 00:12:33.000
to make certain
architectural decisions

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:35.640
or certain challenges
that came your way to make

00:12:35.640 --> 00:12:37.260
that platform a reality.

00:12:37.260 --> 00:12:40.590
Could you throw some lights on
a few challenges that you faced

00:12:40.590 --> 00:12:44.238
and sort of how you had to
overcome it as you went along?

00:12:44.238 --> 00:12:45.030
JEFF TURNHAM: Sure.

00:12:45.030 --> 00:12:45.530
So yeah.

00:12:45.530 --> 00:12:49.290
Because we started
from ground zero,

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:51.810
I'm sure we've encountered
a lot of the same challenges

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:54.370
that any other organization
would encounter,

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.360
especially ones that are
starting from an existing

00:12:57.360 --> 00:13:00.570
portfolio of software and
trying to modernize it or make

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:01.740
it cloud native.

00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:06.420
So we've had to do quite a
lot of internal education

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.153
and enablement on these topics.

00:13:08.153 --> 00:13:09.570
And one thing we
learned early on,

00:13:09.570 --> 00:13:12.330
just to get specific, one
thing we learned early on

00:13:12.330 --> 00:13:16.590
was that it's very
easy to skip over

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.600
some really important
parts of configuration

00:13:18.600 --> 00:13:20.590
when you're deploying
Kubernetes applications.

00:13:20.590 --> 00:13:22.020
So one example.

00:13:22.020 --> 00:13:24.450
Anyone who's deployed
Kubernetes apps in production

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:27.130
will probably understand
this quite well.

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:30.390
But when you deploy a workload,
something that's got a pod,

00:13:30.390 --> 00:13:35.280
the way you set its resource
limits, basically how much CPU

00:13:35.280 --> 00:13:38.700
and memory it can use,
can have a dramatic impact

00:13:38.700 --> 00:13:40.110
on performance.

00:13:40.110 --> 00:13:42.360
And it's just two
little lines usually

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:43.980
or four little lines in YAML.

00:13:43.980 --> 00:13:45.660
But it can have a
tremendous impact

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:48.600
on how your application
actually runs and performs.

00:13:48.600 --> 00:13:49.500
And we saw this.

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:53.340
So we had some of our
existing software that

00:13:53.340 --> 00:13:55.200
got put into a cloud
native form factor,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:56.940
containerized, packaged.

00:13:56.940 --> 00:13:59.310
It all looked good
but, when we ran it,

00:13:59.310 --> 00:14:01.058
it was actually underperforming.

00:14:01.058 --> 00:14:02.850
It was actually performing
much more slowly

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:05.770
than it would if you deployed
onto a VM or elsewhere.

00:14:05.770 --> 00:14:09.570
When we looked into it, the
root cause was CPU throttling.

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:13.020
And so the interesting
part of that

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:15.850
is the fix for that is
usually very simple.

00:14:15.850 --> 00:14:18.270
It's tweaking those
few lines of YAML.

00:14:18.270 --> 00:14:20.692
But the trick is
knowing what values

00:14:20.692 --> 00:14:21.900
to put in in the first place.

00:14:21.900 --> 00:14:26.010
How do I know how much CPU
my workload should request?

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:28.300
How do I know what its
limit's going to be?

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:31.918
And so we, again, opened up
a support ticket with GCP

00:14:31.918 --> 00:14:33.210
to basically ask that question.

00:14:33.210 --> 00:14:34.800
How do we set these values?

00:14:34.800 --> 00:14:37.800
What's the best
practice for doing this?

00:14:37.800 --> 00:14:40.980
The support folks hooked us
up with some Google experts.

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:43.650
We got on a call, had a
chat about this topic.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:46.890
And they basically turned us
on to the power of vertical pod

00:14:46.890 --> 00:14:50.040
autoscaler which, when you run
that tool in recommendation

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:52.360
mode, it effectively answers
the question for you.

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:54.818
It tells you here's what
your CPU settings should be.

00:14:54.818 --> 00:14:56.610
Here's what your memory
settings should be.

00:14:56.610 --> 00:14:58.410
It's basically watching
your application

00:14:58.410 --> 00:15:00.480
and giving your recommendations.

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:03.990
So we've rolled that
out across the org

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:07.200
and we're now focused on making
sure that, as our products

00:15:07.200 --> 00:15:09.455
change, that we keep
up to date with that.

00:15:09.455 --> 00:15:10.830
So we don't want
a product change

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:12.600
to introduce something
that requires

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:14.920
more CPU and our
configuration doesn't change.

00:15:14.920 --> 00:15:16.690
So the question is,
how do you monitor?

00:15:16.690 --> 00:15:19.770
How do you make sure
that you're continuously

00:15:19.770 --> 00:15:21.030
checking and approving that?

00:15:21.030 --> 00:15:24.300
So one thing that we're looking
at for the upcoming year

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:26.520
is, if you think back
to our architecture,

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:28.770
every time a user
deploys something

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.320
to what we call our
sandbox, it deploys

00:15:31.320 --> 00:15:34.440
to one big cluster, that big
sandbox cluster I mentioned.

00:15:34.440 --> 00:15:37.380
And that's got us-- it's
got us a lot of benefits.

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:39.420
And it's got us a long way.

00:15:39.420 --> 00:15:41.940
The one nice thing about
it is it's one cluster

00:15:41.940 --> 00:15:45.180
for our ops team to manage,
and maintain, and monitor.

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:47.700
And so when something goes
wrong, it's very easy to know,

00:15:47.700 --> 00:15:52.445
oh, there's the cluster that
we need to go fix and so on.

00:15:52.445 --> 00:15:53.820
However, there
are some use cases

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:56.580
that we really want to enable
for our end users and business

00:15:56.580 --> 00:16:00.450
partners that we can't really
do or we can't do it robustly

00:16:00.450 --> 00:16:01.800
with this current architecture.

00:16:01.800 --> 00:16:04.470
One of them is, in
addition to just letting

00:16:04.470 --> 00:16:06.630
users have access
to their endpoints,

00:16:06.630 --> 00:16:09.780
the product endpoints
that they can connect to

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.720
in the deployed
application, we actually

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.870
want some of our users to
be able to get Kube admin

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:17.970
rights to be able to
use Kube control to get

00:16:17.970 --> 00:16:20.250
into their cluster and
start poking around

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:22.470
and do things like check
on the status of pods,

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:25.440
maybe delete some pods,
change configuration, things

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.160
like that.

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:28.890
That's not something we'd
been willing to entertain

00:16:28.890 --> 00:16:32.100
with a large cluster, a
large shared cluster, because

00:16:32.100 --> 00:16:35.190
of the security implications
and all of the testing that

00:16:35.190 --> 00:16:36.730
would be required.

00:16:36.730 --> 00:16:39.090
So what we're looking
at is moving to a model

00:16:39.090 --> 00:16:41.313
where, when a user
says they want

00:16:41.313 --> 00:16:43.230
to try out some of our
software, we don't just

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:44.272
deploy their application.

00:16:44.272 --> 00:16:47.820
We actually spin up
dynamically a cluster purpose

00:16:47.820 --> 00:16:49.600
built for that workload.

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:51.990
So it would be sized correctly.

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:54.210
It would have the right
sort of node pools--

00:16:54.210 --> 00:16:56.813
If it's a Windows workload or
it has some Windows in there,

00:16:56.813 --> 00:16:58.230
it would have a
Windows node pool.

00:16:58.230 --> 00:17:00.060
If not, it wouldn't--

00:17:00.060 --> 00:17:03.940
and basically spin these
things up quickly on demand.

00:17:03.940 --> 00:17:06.480
And so we want to move to
that sort of architecture

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:09.000
or at least experiment
and make sure

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:12.000
that that makes sense
and basically get

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:15.900
to a model of much more
dynamic, much more provisioned

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:18.819
infrastructure coming
and going quickly.

00:17:18.819 --> 00:17:20.609
We also want to take
a much closer look

00:17:20.609 --> 00:17:25.770
at GKE Autopilot, which lets
teams like us abstract away

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.710
even further from the concept
of nodes and really just focus

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.620
on our pods and workloads.

00:17:30.620 --> 00:17:32.620
And so we'll be experimenting
with that as well.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:33.210
So yeah.

00:17:33.210 --> 00:17:34.390
Many other examples.

00:17:34.390 --> 00:17:37.230
But across the board, we're
always looking for ways

00:17:37.230 --> 00:17:39.023
to improve our architecture.

00:17:39.023 --> 00:17:41.190
ROMIN IRANI: So the final
thing I wanted to ask you,

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:43.050
and I'm just curious
about this, is

00:17:43.050 --> 00:17:45.690
that for a platform
of this type,

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:48.690
there are different stakeholders
in the organization.

00:17:48.690 --> 00:17:54.600
So could you throw some light on
what it means to be successful

00:17:54.600 --> 00:17:58.293
or how do you measure success
for a platform like this?

00:17:58.293 --> 00:17:59.460
ROMIN IRANI: Great question.

00:17:59.460 --> 00:18:02.490
And this is something
we've been building up

00:18:02.490 --> 00:18:04.530
over time over the
last four years

00:18:04.530 --> 00:18:06.280
that we've been working on this.

00:18:06.280 --> 00:18:10.020
And so the way we see it,
observability and improvement

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:12.940
happens at various
different levels.

00:18:12.940 --> 00:18:15.780
So down at the
technical level, which

00:18:15.780 --> 00:18:19.410
we've been talking about up
till now, for that you obviously

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:21.690
need observability,
logging, monitoring.

00:18:21.690 --> 00:18:25.440
You need someone looking at
those alerts, and those logs,

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.390
and taking action.

00:18:27.390 --> 00:18:32.010
So that's almost the easy part,
because a lot of that logging

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:34.140
and monitoring
functionality is built in.

00:18:34.140 --> 00:18:36.210
What's a little more
challenging, especially

00:18:36.210 --> 00:18:38.850
for large organizations
with thousands of developers

00:18:38.850 --> 00:18:41.610
and dozens and dozens
of software products,

00:18:41.610 --> 00:18:45.930
is how do you move your
whole organization to become

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:50.560
cloud native, both in culture
and how they deliver things?

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:53.610
And so what we've
had to do in HCL

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:58.020
is not just hope and pray
that it happens organically.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:00.060
We actually, at the
executive level,

00:19:00.060 --> 00:19:03.240
have tracking of what we call
our cloud native maturities.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:07.920
We have a cloud native maturity
model that we've built up.

00:19:07.920 --> 00:19:09.840
It's built on a lot
of the best practices

00:19:09.840 --> 00:19:12.507
that you'd go out and find other
people talking about with cloud

00:19:12.507 --> 00:19:14.410
native, but it's
basically a scorecard.

00:19:14.410 --> 00:19:16.320
And it talks about
things like how

00:19:16.320 --> 00:19:17.940
do you build your
containers, both

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:20.850
for security but
also for robustness,

00:19:20.850 --> 00:19:23.700
and logging, and
things like that.

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:26.430
How do you package your
containers into a Kubernetes

00:19:26.430 --> 00:19:28.300
application effectively?

00:19:28.300 --> 00:19:32.070
How do you ensure that the
application's monitorable?

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.600
How do you ensure resilience
and things like that?

00:19:34.600 --> 00:19:38.070
So all of our products actually
get scored on that cloud

00:19:38.070 --> 00:19:39.420
native maturity model.

00:19:39.420 --> 00:19:41.850
And where there's
deficiencies or gaps,

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:43.950
that becomes part of
the product's roadmap.

00:19:43.950 --> 00:19:46.620
And so this, as I said, is
tracked at the executive level.

00:19:46.620 --> 00:19:49.290
It's not left as something
that each product

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:54.180
manager needs to make sure
they carve out time for.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:55.903
And so I think, in a
large organization,

00:19:55.903 --> 00:19:57.570
that's really the way
it has to be done,

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:00.900
if your end goal is
a consistent, cloud

00:20:00.900 --> 00:20:04.270
native evolution across
all of your products.

00:20:04.270 --> 00:20:07.450
I will mention one other thing,
just because it's top of mind.

00:20:07.450 --> 00:20:09.750
We're actually doing
this right now.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:12.240
We're really looking now
at a level of testing

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:13.650
that we've never done before.

00:20:13.650 --> 00:20:16.290
I know other organizations
are far ahead of us on this,

00:20:16.290 --> 00:20:19.740
but we're really looking at
chaos engineering as a way

00:20:19.740 --> 00:20:21.720
to make sure that
our applications are

00:20:21.720 --> 00:20:24.900
resilient against all kinds of
things that can and do go wrong

00:20:24.900 --> 00:20:25.900
in production.

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:30.090
So we are now setting up full
chaos engineering environments

00:20:30.090 --> 00:20:31.740
where we can run
all of our products.

00:20:31.740 --> 00:20:35.460
We can inject-- we're actually
using litmus chaos, open source

00:20:35.460 --> 00:20:39.000
project for this where we
inject all kinds of bad things

00:20:39.000 --> 00:20:43.080
into our cluster, things like
network slowdowns, CPU memory

00:20:43.080 --> 00:20:46.320
throttling, nodes going
bad, pods getting restarted,

00:20:46.320 --> 00:20:48.660
all kinds of this
types of chaos,

00:20:48.660 --> 00:20:51.870
and making sure our products
can handle it, and recover,

00:20:51.870 --> 00:20:55.610
and, in the best case, have
no impact to user experience

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:56.540
during these events.

00:20:56.540 --> 00:20:57.120
Yeah.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:58.620
It's a constant journey.

00:20:58.620 --> 00:21:02.023
And if you ask me four
or five years from now,

00:21:02.023 --> 00:21:03.690
I'm sure we'll still
be on this journey.

00:21:03.690 --> 00:21:05.390
And we'll be thinking about
new things and new ways

00:21:05.390 --> 00:21:06.720
to optimize and improve.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:08.910
So it's never ending.

00:21:08.910 --> 00:21:12.140
ROMIN IRANI: So thank you once
again for sharing your journey

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:17.030
as well as giving us an
overview of how you built out

00:21:17.030 --> 00:21:22.160
SoFy and particularly the whole
journey towards cloud native

00:21:22.160 --> 00:21:25.400
and ensuring that,
along with GCP,

00:21:25.400 --> 00:21:27.440
you are able to build
out this platform.

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:30.380
Thanks for sharing all
your inputs along the way.

00:21:30.380 --> 00:21:32.540
It was a pleasure to
have you on the show.

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:33.590
Thank you so much, Jeff.

00:21:33.590 --> 00:21:34.340
JEFF TURNHAM: Yes.

00:21:34.340 --> 00:21:34.640
Thanks.

00:21:34.640 --> 00:21:36.030
Thanks so much for having me.

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:37.730
ROMIN IRANI: Thank
you for joining us

